description:
memory.rs:340-382 — demo_next_id is a #[cfg]-branched function whose 'realistic-demo-ids' branch ignores all three of its arguments:

  fn demo_next_id(inner: &mut Inner, type_name: &'static str, account_id: &str) -> Id {
      #[cfg(feature = "realistic-demo-ids")]
      {
          use std::sync::atomic::{AtomicU64, Ordering};
          use std::sync::OnceLock;
          use std::time::{SystemTime, UNIX_EPOCH};

          let _ = (inner, type_name, account_id);     // <-- triple discard
          static COUNTER: AtomicU64 = AtomicU64::new(0);
          ...

The triple-binding discard 'let _ = (inner, type_name, account_id);' is a cosmetic blemish. It moves three values into a tuple solely to drop them. Idiomatic Rust handles cfg-conditional unused params with the underscore-prefix-on-param-name convention or with cfg-attr:

(a) Underscore-prefix the param names that are unused in this branch:
       #[cfg(feature = "realistic-demo-ids")]
       fn demo_next_id(_inner: &mut Inner, _type_name: &'static str, _account_id: &str) -> Id { ... }
       #[cfg(not(feature = "realistic-demo-ids"))]
       fn demo_next_id(inner: &mut Inner, type_name: &'static str, account_id: &str) -> Id { ... }
   This splits the function into two cfg-branched implementations; clearer at the cost of repetition.

(b) Annotate the function with cfg_attr:
       #[cfg_attr(feature = "realistic-demo-ids", allow(unused_variables))]
       fn demo_next_id(inner: &mut Inner, type_name: &'static str, account_id: &str) -> Id {
           ...
       }
   Single signature, lint-allowance scoped to the right cfg.

Either is preferable to silently dropping three values via a tuple. The current form also has a subtle issue: 'let _ = (inner, ...)' may or may not move the values (depending on Copy-ness), and for a future maintainer this looks like 'these values are being saved for later use' more than 'these are deliberately discarded'.

Cosmetic / clippy-level; behaviour-identical.
